# Game Architecture

Organize by feature, not by leftover type dumps. Typical top-level folders: `scenes/`, `entities/`, `systems/`, `assets/`.

## Structure

- Keep engine, scene management, and entity/systems as separate modules.
- Centralize assets in one loader/service. Centralize storage (saves, settings) behind one API.
- Put game config and physics numbers in one constants module. Use env vars for development, staging, and production.
- Avoid ad-hoc globals. If shared state is needed, use one state system (Observer / Command / State as they fit).
- Handle native plugins (audio, device) in one service.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
window.playerHp = 10;
PIXI.Assets.load('hero.png'); // scattered loads

// ✅ GOOD
import { GAME } from './constants';
import { assetService } from './assets/asset-service';
import { storage } from './storage/storage';

const texture = await assetService.loadTexture('hero');
storage.set('settings', { sfxVolume: GAME.DEFAULT_SFX_VOLUME });
```

## Quality

- Unit-test game logic and systems; integration-test scenes. Mock native/network APIs.
- Profile on target devices. Add automated performance checks in CI to catch regressions.
- Use global error handlers and a crash reporter in production.
- Cross-device test (web + Capacitor). Watch browser-specific Pixi/WebGL quirks.
